schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Local Currency Design for Community Finance
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A practical guide to designing and launching a community currency: what
    unit to use (time-based, value-based, points-based), what backs it
    (nothing/fiat, labour, goods, national currency), who issues it
    (member-issued, system-issued, hybrid), what rules to set (earning,
    spending, expiry, fees), and technical implementation choices (paper
    ledgers, shared digital ledger, blockchain). Covers a three-phase launch
    process (design, pilot, scale), common mistakes, and named case studies
    (Ithaca Hours, Brixton Pound, Sardex, BerkShares, and East African
    chamas/ROSCAs as an existing informal precedent to build on).
  audience: local-node organizers, East Africa-focused track (Somaliland, Kenya, Tanzania context)
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      keeping value local (the core purpose of a local currency) vs. tying
      the unit to a national currency (value-based backing), which
      re-imports the inflation/instability the currency is meant to buffer
      against
  related_concepts:
    - Local / Community Currency
  related_resources:
    - Ithaca Hours
    - Brixton Pound
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/local-currency.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

# assessed-but-excluded:
#   - Sardex, BerkShares, and Chamas/ROSCAs were named as case studies within
#     the concept-lineage's source_traditions rather than each given a
#     standalone resource object — only the two most historically cited
#     examples (Ithaca Hours, Brixton Pound) were lifted separately, to keep
#     this batch proportionate.
#   - The blockchain-based implementation option was captured in the guide
#     summary/concept-lineage rather than split into its own claim-evidence
#     — the article itself is cautious/non-committal about it ("almost no
#     new community currencies" need it), so there is no strong claim to
#     evidence-check.
